---
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
import ReleaseBlock from "../components/ReleaseBlock.astro";
import StatusBadge from "../components/StatusBadge.astro";
import { appBySlug, repoUrl } from "../data/apps";
import { archiveFolder, assetByKind } from "../data/release-model";
import { releaseFor } from "../data/releases";
import { url } from "../paths";

const app = appBySlug("ftp");
const release = releaseFor("ftp");
const archive = release ? assetByKind(release, "archive") : undefined;
const repo = repoUrl;
---

<h1 class="app-title">
  {app.icon && <img src={url(app.icon)} width="48" height="48" alt="" />}
  {app.name}
</h1>
<StatusBadge app={app} release={release} />
<p>
  A dual-pane file-transfer app. This computer on one side, the server on the other, and a
  transfer queue. It is written in Rust, draws with egui, and does not use GTK or Qt. The window
  is also the connection manager for the terminal: saved connections work from the command line
  and from plain ssh.
</p>
<p>
  {release?.prerelease && "Early alpha. "}
  {release ? (
    <>
      The latest download is <a href={release.htmlUrl}>{release.version}</a>, published {release.dateLabel}.
      {release.prerelease && " GitHub marks it as a pre-release."}
    </>
  ) : (
    <>No release is published yet.</>
  )}
  {" "}This version speaks SFTP. FTP and FTPS addresses can be saved. Opening them is not built yet.
  {release?.prerelease && " Things may still change or break. Try it on servers and files you have backups of."}
</p>


<nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page">
  <a href="#window">The window</a>
  <a href="#transfers">Transfers</a>
  <a href="#editing">Editing</a>
  <a href="#ssh">SSH keys</a>
  <a href="#connections">Saved connections</a>
  <a href="#install">Install</a>
</nav>
<p>How to do each of these, the terminal commands, and the keys are in the <a href={url("docs/ftp/")}>FTP docs</a>.</p>

<h2 id="window">The window</h2>
<p>
  Saved connections sit in groups in the sidebar. Tabs hold several servers at once. Each tab has
  its own panes and queue, and a tab in the background keeps transferring. A quick-connect bar
  takes an address without saving it.
</p>
<p>
  Both panes browse on their own, with Rivet's stepped path bar and columns you can sort by name,
  size, or date. The toolbar can be arranged, and the menu bar can be hidden. The window follows
  the desktop's colors, fonts, and icon theme, in light or dark.
</p>
<Shot
  name="ftp-connected-wide.png"
  alt="Rivet FTP in the pink scheme, connected, with this computer and the server side by side"
  caption="Connected, with finished transfers. The pictures use a demo server made of a local folder."
  eager
/>
<Shot
  name="ftp-light-wide.png"
  alt="Rivet FTP in the light theme, with both panes showing the same site"
  caption="The same window in the light theme."
/>
<Shot
  name="ftp-new-connection-wide.png"
  alt="The New Connection window, with SFTP selected"
  caption="A new connection. It is saved for the window and for the terminal."
/>

<h2 id="transfers">Transfers</h2>
<p>
  Files and whole folders go into a queue, by drag and drop or with Upload and Download. A
  download shows how much has moved, the speed, and the time left. A finished row shows the size,
  the time, and the speed. A failed transfer can be retried.
</p>
<p>
  When a file is already on the other side, the dates and sizes are compared before anything is
  replaced, and you choose: keep it, replace the older files, or replace all of them. Pause and
  resume, a bandwidth limit, several transfers at once, and folder sync are still on the list.
</p>

<div class="pair">
  <Shot
    name="ftp-replace-wide.png"
    alt="A dialog comparing two items that are already on the server"
    caption="Already on the server. Dates and sizes are compared before anything is replaced."
  />
  <Shot
    name="ftp-preferences-wide.png"
    alt="Preferences, Transfers: ask, replace, or skip when a file is already there"
    caption="Preferences, Transfers. Ask, replace, or skip, and which editor opens a server file."
  />
</div>

<h2 id="editing">Editing and file info</h2>
<p>
  Open a file on the server in your own editor. Rivet FTP downloads it to a private folder, opens
  it, and sends each save back. If the server copy changed in the meantime, it asks before
  replacing it.
</p>
<p>
  Info shows size, date, owner, and permissions, and can change permissions for one file or
  everything in a folder. New folder, rename, and delete work on the server, and delete asks
  first. Pictures and text can be previewed.
</p>
<Shot
  name="ftp-info-wide.png"
  alt="Info for a file on the server, with owner, size, and permission checkboxes"
  caption="Info and permissions for a file on the server."
/>

<h2 id="ssh">SSH keys</h2>
<p>
  SFTP goes through the system's own sftp program, so your keys, ssh agent, ssh config, and known
  hosts all apply. A new server is trusted on first use. A changed server key is refused.
</p>
<p>
  Preferences lists your SSH keys, makes new ones, copies a public key, and adds one to a server.
  Each connection picks its key. Private keys never leave your computer.
</p>
<Shot
  name="ftp-keys-wide.png"
  alt="Preferences, SSH Keys, in the light theme"
  caption="Preferences, SSH Keys, in the light theme. Add to Server sends the public half."
/>

<h2 id="connections">Saved connections</h2>
<p>
  A connection saved in the window also works from a terminal: log in over ssh, open the sftp
  command line, or reach the server by name from ssh, scp, rsync, and git. Connections are kept
  in one plain text file you can read and edit.
</p>
<p>
  Passwords are never written into that file. If you ask the window to remember a password or a
  key passphrase, it goes in the desktop keyring.
</p>

<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
<ReleaseBlock slug="ftp" />
{
  archive && (
    <>
      <p>
        Unpack the archive and run <code>install.sh</code>. That puts <code>rivet-ftp</code> in
        <code>~/.local/bin</code> and adds Rivet FTP to the app launcher.
        {!release?.assets.some((item) => item.kind === "appimage" || item.kind === "flatpak") &&
          " There is no AppImage or Flatpak for this release."}
      </p>
      <pre>
        <code>{`tar xzf ${archive.name}\ncd ${archiveFolder(archive.name)}\n./install.sh`}</code>
      </pre>
    </>
  )
}
<p>
  The program is a portable x86_64 build, linked against glibc 2.31. The window needs
  <a href={url("docs/files/#installing")}>the same libraries as Rivet Files</a>.
</p>
<h3>From source</h3>
<p>
  Rust 1.88 or newer. From the top of the repository,
  <code>utilities/rivet-dev install ftp</code> builds a release and adds the launcher entry. Or:
</p>
<pre><code>cargo build --release -p rivet-ftp
./target/release/rivet-ftp
./target/release/rivet-ftp open work-site
./target/release/rivet-ftp --help
./target/release/rivet-ftp --install-desktop-entry</code></pre>
<p>
  Still ahead, from the feature checklist: opening FTP and FTPS, pause and resume, more than one
  transfer at a time, folder sync, reconnecting after a drop, and Rivet's icon and compact views
  inside the panes. The checklist is
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/rivet-ftp-parity.md`}>docs/rivet-ftp-parity.md</a>.
</p>

<p>
  Longer notes: <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/rivet-ftp.md`}>docs/rivet-ftp.md</a>,
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-ftp/README.md`}>apps/rivet-ftp</a>, and the
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-ftp/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a>.
</p>
